                'content' => '<p>A design system is more than a component library. It\'s the single source of truth that keeps a dozen teams shipping interfaces that feel like one product. Here are the lessons I\'ve learned building them at enterprise scale.</p><h2>Tokens Before Components</h2><p>Start with design tokens — color, type, spacing, radius. Everything else derives from them. When tokens are the foundation, theming and dark mode become configuration, not surgery.</p><h2>One Source of Truth</h2><p>Components must live in exactly one package and be consumed everywhere. Versioned releases with changelogs let teams upgrade on their own schedule without breaking each other.</p><h2>Documentation Is the Product</h2><p>Without great docs, your system becomes a rumor. Every component needs live examples, prop tables, and guidance on when to use it — and just as importantly, when not to.</p><h2>Governance Over Process</h2><p>Decide how changes get in: contribution guidelines, a review board, and a clear deprecation path. A system without governance decays into a zoo of one-off components.</p><p>Invest early, and the payoff compounds: faster shipping, fewer bugs, and interfaces users actually recognize.</p>',
