                'content' => '<p>Next.js 14\'s App Router is a paradigm shift. Server Components run on the server by default, giving you faster loads and smaller bundles, while the rest of the React ecosystem keeps working the way you expect.</p><h2>Server Components by Default</h2><p>Move data fetching and rendering to the server, where it belongs. Your client bundle shrinks, your page loads faster, and you can safely use secrets and databases in components without exposing them.</p><h2>Streaming with Suspense</h2><p>Stream every part of your page the moment it\'s ready. Wrap slow, non-critical sections in Suspense boundaries and let the fast parts render first — no more blank screens waiting on one slow query.</p><h2>Parallel & Intercepting Routes</h2><p>Parallel routes render multiple independent views in the same layout — perfect for dashboards. Intercepting routes power patterns like modals that deep-link to their own URL, keeping shareability intact.</p><h2>The New Metadata API</h2><p>Exporting metadata and generateMetadata gives you full control over SEO tags, Open Graph, and canonical URLs per route, statically or dynamically.</p><p>Adopting the App Router is a learning curve, but it pays for itself in performance and developer experience.</p>',
