                'content' => '<p>Micro-interactions are the secret sauce of delightful interfaces. A button that responds the moment it\'s pressed, a card that lifts as you hover, a success state that feels earned — these details separate memorable products from functional ones.</p><h2>Design the Motion, Then Code It</h2><p>Great animation is intentional. Define easing curves that match your brand, keep durations short (150–300ms is the sweet spot), and always respect prefers-reduced-motion for users who need stillness.</p><h2>Transform and Opacity Only</h2><p>The golden rule of performance: animate only transform and opacity. The browser composites those on the GPU. Animating width, height, or top triggers expensive layout and paint on every frame.</p><h2>Easing Makes or Breaks It</h2><p>Default ease feels robotic. Cubic-bezier curves that start fast and ease out — like the popular ease-out-quint — make motion feel physical and satisfying.</p><h2>Micro-interaction Patterns That Work</h2><p>Hover lifts, pressed states, animated feedback on form validation, skeleton loaders, and staggered entrances. Each one guides attention and makes the interface feel alive and responsive.</p><p>Start small: pick one interaction, make it feel great, then move to the next.</p>',
